1,2 $ Pileus and stipe squamose.
1,3 $ PiIeus and stipe not squamose.
2,0 $ Spores 5.5-8.5 x 2.5-3.5 um; squamules on pileus narrow, distinctly darker than context; lamellae with hyphal pegs; growing on wood of deciduous trees $ L. tigrinus 1
2,0 $ Spores 8.0-12.5 x 3.5-5.0 um; squamules on pileus, at least at centre, very broad and ± concolorous with surface; hyphal pegs absent; growing on wood of conifers. $ L. lepideus 4
3,4 $ . Pleurocystidia absent; lamellae deeply decurrent; spores 4.0-4.5 um wide or spores narrower but then smell strongly of anise.
3,5 $ Pleurocystidia present; lamellae sinuate, subdecurrent or decurrent; spores up to 3.5 um wide; never with smell of anise.
4,0 $ Spores 10.0-12.0 x 4.0-4.5/lm; pileus from ± 30 to 100 mm and more in diam., ochraceous with crowded small brown granu1ar dots; smell pleasant or farinaceous but not like anise $ L. cyathiformis  5
4,0 $ Spores 6.0-9.0 x 2.5-3.5/lm; pileus from 5-50 mm, pale yellow to ochraceous brown and glabrous; smell strong1y of anise $ L. suavissimus Fr.
4,0,n$. Syn. Gen. Lentinorum: 13.1836;  mainly in eastern Europe, but also in Sweden, Germany, and France; mostly on Salix, rarely on Populus, in marshy forest. Not in the Netherlands.
5,0 $ PiIeus glabrous, reddish-lilacinous; lamella edge concolorous; pleurocystidia clavate, metuloid, 40-65/lm long; spores 5.0-6.0 x 2.5-3.5/lm; growing on wood of deciduous trees $  L. conchatus  2
5,0 $ Pileus villose, greyish beige to yellowish brown; lamella edge yellowish; pleurocystidia lageniform, thin-walled, 60-125 um long; spores 7.0-11.5 x 3.0-3.5/lm; growing on wood of conifers $  L. adhaerens  3
